# Which preset covers which annotations, and what each one is for.
#
# Pipe separated rather than JSON, because the script that reads it is `/bin/sh` and this repository ships
# no `jq`. A JSON index would mean either a dependency an install cannot assume or a parser written in shell,
# and a parser in the Skill is the thing this whole boundary exists to avoid. Four fields, no nesting, and
# nothing here needs more.
#
# name | document | annotations it covers | what it describes
#
# The annotation names are what a build reports as *uninterpreted*. That is how a preset is chosen: the
# Engine says which annotations it saw and did not read, and a name in this column means a preset exists for
# them. Matching on the Engine's own report rather than on a framework name is deliberate — naming the
# framework would need a list of frameworks this build knows of and cannot read, which the root PRD's
# section 4 forbids by another route.

jpa | jpa.nost | Entity,Table,Column,Id,GeneratedValue,ManyToOne,OneToMany,OneToOne,ManyToMany,JoinColumn,Embeddable,Embedded,Transient | persistence mappings: entities, the columns they hold, and the associations between them
